{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And he pierced both of them, etc.</b> It is difficult: granted, the piercing of the Israelite man is according to the halakhic ruling that zealots may strike him; but the woman is not liable to death and is not commanded, and if it was out of suspicion that she might be a married woman, she would not be killed on a doubtful basis. And it is possible that he judged her by the law of the beast, as it is written (Leviticus 20:15–16): ״וְאֶת הַבְּהֵמָה תַּהֲרֹגוּ״, and it is written (Ezekiel 23:20): ״אֲשֶׁר בְּשַׂר חֲמוֹרִים בְּשָׂרָם״." "And if you say: why did Israel not kill the Midianite woman beforehand, since they were a people concerning whom they had not been commanded? It is possible that it is because Israel had not yet sent them an offer of peace (Deuteronomy 20:10); and even though in the end they also did not send to them, that was after the fact, for HaShem commanded ״צָרוֹר״ etc. (Numbers 25:17). Or perhaps they did not recognize her and thought she was a Moabite woman and not a Midianite, since they were not accustomed to identifying them, or her garb was like the garb of Moab. And the verse's saying ״the Midianite woman״ is the language of the verse." "<b>Thus concludes the portion of Balak.</b>"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:14:11.582923397Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}